the last video, you need to first
understand what correlation actually is.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
And correlation is basically just a
statistical measure that tries to quantify
the extent to which two variables
fluctuate together.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Or put simply, it tries to identify how
the two variables are related in a linear
fashion.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.000
There are various measures of correlation,
but the most common is called the Pearson
coefficient of correlation.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:21.000
And when I talk about correlation in this
video or in the course in general, this is
really the statistical measure that I'm
referring to.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.000
The Pearson coefficient of correlation is
a pairwise correlation value that measures
the linear relationship between two
variables.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
And I've got the formula up here.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.000
And that isn't something you need to worry
about remembering, but what is useful is
just to have a general understanding of
how that formula works.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Given that you're going to be calculating
and using correlation figures in the
spreadsheets that we're looking at and
providing to you to help monitor and
manage your own portfolios.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:08.000
So one of the major misunderstandings of
correlation is that people think that if
the two variables that they're looking at
both have positive changes in their data,
it means that the relationship or
correlation between them must also be
positive.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:11.000
And unfortunately, that's the incorrect
assumption to make.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:24.000
And in actual fact, what happens is that
for every observation of both the X and Y
variables that you're looking at, they're
converted and normalized to values that
represent their distances from the mean.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Not dissimilar, actually, to the
methodology that we looked at in the
previous video with the standard deviation
calculation.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Anyway, those values are then compared to
each other to compute an average
relationship over the entire data set.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:49.000
And that number can then be interpreted to
answer whether the variables are
positively or negatively related and also
how strong that relationship is.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Speaking of the possible outputs of the
correlation equation, another thing you
ought to know is that correlation can take
any value between 1 and minus 1 or 100%
and minus 100%.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:18.000
A value of 1 or 100% represents a
perfectly positive linear relationship
between two variables and a value of minus
1, which is the same as minus 100%, is a
perfectly negative linear relationship.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:29.000
A correlation of 0 suggests that the
variables that you're looking at are
perfectly uncorrelated and there is no
linear relationship between them.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:39.000
However, that doesn't mean that the
variables are independent from each other
because they could, of course, still have
a relationship with each other that isn't
defined in a linear fashion.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:48.000
So let's have a look at some examples of
what various levels of correlation look
like in the form of scatterplot charts.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Now, if you don't really understand what
scatterplot charts are, you don't need to
worry about that too much at the moment.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
You'll soon figure it out from the next
slide and then you can obviously sort of
rewind back to this slide if needed.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Essentially though, all I'm trying to
point out here at this stage is that if
there is an upward sloping relationship,
so from bottom left to top right, between
the two variables, then that represents a
positive correlation.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.000
And a downward sloping relationship
represents a negative correlation.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:27.000
The dispersion of the plots represent the
strength of the relationship.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:35.000
And you can see in the top row here, the
more dispersed the data points are, the
closer to zero the correlation value
becomes.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:43.000
And the gradient or the slope of the data
points is actually irrelevant to the
strength of relationships.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:49.000
The strength of relationships is defined
by the dispersion, as I said, not the
gradient of the slope.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:55.000
And you can see that in the bottom line of
plots as well.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:06.000
Okay, let's look at a real example now
using real data that everyone's familiar
with so that you can understand, or at
least everyone sort of understands.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:13.000
What we're looking at here is a
scatterplot showing the relationship
between major league baseball players'
height and weight.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Anecdotally, of course, we would expect
weight and height to have a positive
relationship,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
which means taller people tend to be
heavier and vice versa.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.000
In this chart, height is measured in
inches for each player.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.000
And that's plotted on the x-axis, which is
the horizontal axis.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.000
And weight is measured in pounds for each
player.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.000
And that's plotted on the y-axis, which is
the vertical axis.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Each dot on the chart is a data point that
has a value for both height and weight.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.000
And of course, those dots represent the
major league baseball players from this
sample of data.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.000
So what can we analyze from this chart?

00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Well, first off, we can see the heaviest
player weighs in around 290 pounds and the
lightest players weigh around 150 pounds.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.000
The tallest player measures 83 inches,
whilst the shortest players are around 67
inches.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
And you can see that a line of best fit
has been drawn through all of these data
points.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.000
And formally, this line is calculated by
minimizing the distances between all of
the data points in the chart.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:27.000
But you can just think of this really as
the sort of average relationship between
the variables height and weight.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.000
The majority of the data points that we
can see are clustered in the middle of the
chart,

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:39.000
which makes perfect sense because most
people obviously have a fairly average
height and weight by definition.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:45.000
And if you know anything about normal
distributions, then that's going to make
perfect sense to you.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:52.000
So anyway, when you calculate the
correlation for this data, it comes out as
0.53,

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.000
which is quite a strong positive linear
relationship.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.000
And you can see that reflected in the
chart here.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.000
As players height sort of deviates from
the mean, so does their weight in the same
direction typically.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:10.000
So we have this upward sloping
relationship with not too much dispersion,

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.000
hence quite a strong correlation value of
