their portfolios.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:27.000
The third and final useful sort of way to
use correlation is through diversification
and risk in the portfolio.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.000
And really, that's the most important use
of correlation analysis.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.000
And that's our focus as traders and
portfolio managers.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:40.000
And I've talked about this in the previous
video and we're going to carry on with
that here.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:47.000
Now, you guys have seen this chart already
and I talked through the principles behind
it in the previous video.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:52.000
It is important though, so I'm going to go
through things briefly again here.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:59.000
This chart really sums up why correlation
between positions in your portfolio is so
important to consider.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:08.000
It basically shows us the benefits of
diversification or really what I like to
call efficient diversification.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:15.000
For those of you that don't know what
diversification really means, it's
basically the process of pulling assets
together

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:23.000
that have diverse drivers or factors that
affect their values so that the risk of
the whole portfolio is reduced.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:32.000
And as a reminder, in this chart, what
we're looking at are various portfolios
where each colored line represents a
different portfolio.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
And in each of these portfolios, the
constituent assets are assumed to have
equal capital weightings,

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:45.000
equal volatility values and equal pairwise
correlations to all of the other positions
in the portfolio.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:55.000
Obviously, in reality, these assumptions
are far fetched, but they allow us to
understand the theory of portfolio
diversification much more easily.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:04.000
What I'm really trying to show in this
chart is that there are two core factors
that affect an equally weighted
portfolio's volatility, as you know.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:10.000
The volatility of the assets in the
portfolio and the correlations between
those assets.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:17.000
Now, knowing that, the next question we
should have is, well, how much do each of
these factors have an effect?

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.000
And the answer is that it depends on the
size of the portfolio.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:29.000
What we can see in this chart is that as
assets are added to the portfolios, as
long as the correlation of those positions
is less than one,

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:34.000
one, the risk or volatility of the
portfolio falls quite significantly.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:42.000
The lower the correlation, the more
efficient the diversification process
becomes and the faster the risk decreases.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:50.000
So, for example, the blue and orange
portfolios you can see both have exactly
the same asset volatility assumptions.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:59.000
And so, when there's only one asset in the
portfolio, the volatility of the portfolio
is the same, 35% on an annualized basis.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:07.000
However, as assets are added to those
portfolios, the volatility of the orange
portfolio reduces at a much faster rate

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:18.000
because the correlation values assumed
between the constituents in that portfolio
are set to 0.2 as opposed to 0.45 in the
blue portfolio.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Now, let's consider that question that I
mentioned previously again.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:36.000
When portfolios contain just a very small
number of assets, for example, one or two,
then the volatilities of the individual
assets are quite important to overall
portfolio volatility.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:43.000
However, in any reasonably sized
portfolio, it's the correlation values
that matter much, much more.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:52.000
And we did see this in the previous video
when I was looking at the variance
covariance matrix and kind of running
through examples there.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:55.000
But you can also see it in this chart
here.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:07.000
So, on that point, you might be asking
next whether it's a good idea to
continually add positions that have
relatively low correlations to the
portfolio to keep reducing risk further
and further.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:13.000
And as I've also said previously, the
simple answer to that is a resounding no.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.000
And that is for two main reasons.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:28.000
One of the primary measures of good
trading is achieving what's called good
risk adjusted returns, meaning your
portfolio generates good returns for
relatively little risk over time.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.000
And that can be measured in a few ways.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:39.000
We've already touched on the Sharpe ratio,
and we're going to look at that amongst
some other performance metrics as well
later in the series.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:50.000
If you take a look at the chart here,
you'll see that there comes a point for
every portfolio where adding new positions
doesn't really affect the overall
portfolio volatility.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:57.000
The lower the correlations are of the new
positions, the more risk is reduced.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:03.000
But that being said, the marginal
reduction in risk always diminishes with
every new position.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:17.000
And there comes a point usually between
sort of eight to 12 positions, sometimes a
bit more, where there becomes very little
benefit in terms of reduced portfolio
volatility.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:25.000
Bearing that in mind, let's think about
the return side of our risk adjusted
returns metrics.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:33.000
When building a portfolio, it's not
unreasonable to think that the number of
high quality trade ideas anyone can come
up with is finite.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:40.000
High quality, high conviction ideas are
the positions that generate the most
returns in your portfolios.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:48.000
And when you add more and more positions
to the portfolio, it's likely that the
quality of those ideas will diminish.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:15:00.000
So not only does adding more and more
positions to the portfolio for the sake of
diversification have diminishing benefits
in terms of risk reduction, it also
reduces your expected returns.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.000
This process is what's known as over
diversification and it can dramatically
decrease your portfolio performance.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:19.000
Typically, depending on the amount of
capital you have to trade, you're likely
going to be looking at having around eight
to 12 positions on any one time in the
portfolio.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:31.000
As I said in the previous video, that
might increase a little bit depending on
the sort of total capital you have
available and obviously the sort of market
situation and so on.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:40.000
To summarize this slide, we can see how
important efficient diversification and
correlation is in our portfolios.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:44.000
However, we do need to be cognizant of
taking things too far.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:54.000
Always make sure that in your sort of
prospective portfolios, positions are
driven primarily by fundamental factors.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:59.000
And only then should you think about
correlation and portfolio construction.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.000
And we'll talk more on that shortly.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:10.000
Okay, before going on to some practical
spreadsheet stuff, I want to go through a
few pitfalls of correlation analysis.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:23.000
And this isn't to put you off by any
means, but it is there to give you a
deeper understanding of how correlation
works and what you might need to consider
or look out for if you're using
correlation values in your own analysis or
portfolio.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:34.000
The first one we've already covered, over
diversification can lead to lower returns,
both in sort of absolute and risk adjusted
terms.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:40.000
The second point I want to make is that
correlation is not the same as causation.

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:52.000
What I mean by that is just because there
might be a linear relationship between two
variables, however strong, it doesn't mean
that there is a cause and effect
relationship between them.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:58.000
A change in one might not cause a change
in the other.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:09.000
If there isn't a cause and effect
relationship, then without understanding
the cause factor for a correlation, then
there is going to be uncertainty that the
correlation will stay constant.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:21.000
If we think about the previous video and
our own portfolios, that obviously has
implications for using assumptions of
covariance or correlations in our model.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:37.000
We have to appreciate that these variables
are all subject to change, so that doesn't
make them redundant, it just means we're
trying to work with the data that we have
available, but we need to be aware that
that data isn't perfect.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:50.000
Ideally, when it comes to building
portfolios, it would be optimal to build
them with positions that have very
different fundamental drivers so that we
can try and assure that correlations
remain low going forward.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:00.000
However, it's impossible to know all of
the factors that affect assets, and if you
thought you could get close, then they
would probably change over time anyway.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.000
A classic example of how correlations can
change over time is seen in the
relationship between US stocks and bonds.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:24.000
This chart here shows the rolling
five-year correlation between 10-year
treasury prices and the S&P 500, which you
can see pre-1998 was quite strongly
positive, but in the next five years
turned negative and has stayed that way
ever since.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:30.000
I'm not going to go into details as to why
I think that's happened, because it's
besides the point.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:39.000
But what's noteworthy is that it has
happened, and it's a great example of how
correlations can break down or even
invert.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:46.000
Next one, this might sound silly to you
guys, but correlation really is just a
number.

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:54.000
It captures the linear relationship
between two variables in a very specific
way and based on the data in the sample
that it's calculated from.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:19:07.000
It's very easy to misinterpret, and if you
really want to understand the relationship
between data, then you're going to have to
do more than just look at some descriptive
statistics and correlations.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:13.000
And Anscombe's Quartet is a classic
example of this.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:21.000
So Anscombe's Quartet consists of four
different data sets, all with the same
core descriptive characteristics.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:33.000
They have the same average, variance,
correlations, regression equations, and so
on, which might make you think that these
data sets are clearly very, very similar.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.000
However, in this case, you'd be wrong in
that assumption.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:40.000
And you can see here the differences in
the data sets are massive.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:48.000
The point here is that correlation, like
any other statistic, is just a number, and
you need to understand when it's useful
and when it isn't.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:55.000
And often to do that, you'll have to use
other investigative methods to really
understand the data itself.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:58.000
I mentioned that here really for two
reasons.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:04.000
Firstly, to warn you of the limitations of
certain statistics when taken in and of
themselves.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:16.000
And secondly, to encourage you to go a
little bit further in your own work and
analysis, because that's going to give you
more confidence in the outcomes that you
then reach.

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:31.000
Another very common mistake, and one I
mentioned earlier in the video, is
assuming that if two data sets are
trending in the same direction, then their
correlation must be positive and vice
versa.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:43.000
If you remember back to the equation, it's
calculated based on the relative
difference in each observation from the
mean of the data set for each variable.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:47.000
But the means of each data set themselves
could be very different.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:57.000
So for example, if the mean return of
asset X is minus 1% and the mean return of
asset Y is plus 1% over all observations.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:13.000
But for every sort of individual
observation, when asset X returns above
the mean above minus 1% and asset Y
returns above its mean above 1%, then
there will be a very strong positive
correlation.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:19.000
This concept is probably more easily
illustrated through an example.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:31.000
And here I've got a chart showing the
price of a real estate ETF, IYR is the
ticker, versus an inverse real estate ETF,
REK, over the year 2020.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:39.000
And as you would sort of predict, the
correlation of these instruments is almost
perfectly negative, it's minus 0.99.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:47.000
However, if you take the trend in price of
both of those funds over that year, they
actually both go down.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:22:01.000
Meaning, if you bought both at the start
of the year 2020, expecting a perfect
hedge, you would have actually lost money
on both sides of the trade, despite a near
perfect negative correlation.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:11.000
The lesson here is, remember that
correlation works by comparing data points
to the means of the sort of X and Y
variables.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:21.000
So the means of the variables themselves
can be very different and still yield
perfect correlations.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:28.000
The final pitfall that I want to talk
about, which I mentioned briefly earlier
as well, is common to many statistics.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:36.000
And that is that the calculation itself,
the correlation is dependent obviously on
the data set that's used.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:42.000
And can of course vary for the same two
variables by looking at different samples.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:47.000
So for example, using different sort of
time periods or frequencies of data.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:52.000
We've seen what happens to the correlation
of bonds and stocks already.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:23:01.000
But another very important situation to
look at is what happens in crises when
