00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:37.000
So when we calculate correlations, we base
it on returns rather than prices.

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:42.000
And so to begin with, we need to calculate
all the weekly returns given our price
data.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:46.000
And then we can move on to the correlation
function itself.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:50.000
Now, as you guys know by now, calculating
returns is pretty easy.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:53.000
And I'm just going to do that in columns D
and E quickly.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:27:00.000
So we just take the current price divided
by the previous price minus one.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.000
That's our formula.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:08.000
And then we copy down that formula for
each stock in order to generate our series
of returns.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:19.000
As always, when we do that, we'll be left
with errors at the beginning of the data
series since returns on a given date
require prices from the previous period.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:24.000
And at the start of the series, there's no
previous periods to look up prices for.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:32.000
So we just delete the contents of those
cells and our return series becomes one
period shorter than our price series.

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:38.000
So with returns computed, we can now look
at calculating correlation.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:44.000
The function that we're going to use here
to calculate correlation in Excel is just
called CORAL.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.000
So that's equals CORAL.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:53.000
And if I type that in cell H1, you'll see
that within the function, there are two
parameters to assign.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:55.000
So equals CORAL open brackets.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:00.000
And we can see that there's two parameters
to assign basically array one and array
two.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.000
And these are just your references to your
X and Y variable data sets.

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:11.000
So that's just going to be the returns for
WMS and OGS in this example.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:14.000
It doesn't matter which data series you
enter first.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:20.000
But here what I'm going to do is just
select all of our WMS returns data as the
first parameter.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:27.000
And then enter a comma to move on to array
two and select all of the OGS returns
data.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:37.000
We're selecting all of the data available
at this point because we want to calculate
the historic correlation over the entire
data set in this first example.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:50.000
One thing to note here when using the
correlation function function in Excel is
that you must always use data series that
are aligned and have the same number of
data points.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:52.000
Otherwise, it's not going to work.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:59.000
So the arrays, the reference arrays for
both sort of parameters, both variables
need to be exactly the same length.

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.000
And if they're not the same length, it
won't work.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:14.000
And then obviously for your own benefit,
you want to make sure that all of the
prices or whatever data that you're
looking at matches in each row as well for
the dates that you're considering.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:21.000
So with that data entered, you can see the
correlation measures roughly 0.27 over the
whole data set.

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:35.000
Let's have a look now at rolling
correlations where we're going to use
