00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:42.000
So in column F, I'm going to head the
column one year role coral.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:48.000
And in cell F2, we're going to use the
same correlation function equals coral.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:57.000
But this time when selecting the two
return series, I'm going to make sure that
the arrays only account for the last year
of data.

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:11.000
So in this example, that means putting in
equals coral open brackets and we're going
to select from cells D2 to D54 for the
first array and cells E2 to E54 for the
second array.

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:21.000
The reason why we're going to row 54 is
because that then accounts for 52 weeks of
data and that is equivalent to one year.

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:33.000
So over the latest year of data, you can
see, and I say latest year of data because
we're looking firstly in the top row at
the most recent data that we have
available.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:42.000
So over the latest year of data, you can
see the correlation has measured much less
than our sort of overall correlation for
the entire data set.

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:53.000
So the reading is 0.04 and that shows
