structuring?

00:58:53.500 --> 00:59:02.500
In the last video, we learned that a
portfolio's variance is a function of both
the variances and covariances of
individual assets within the portfolio.

00:59:02.500 --> 00:59:06.500
However, we found that as more assets are
added to the portfolio,

00:59:06.500 --> 00:59:13.500
it's the covariance terms that become the
dominant contributors to the eventual
output of portfolio volatility.

00:59:13.500 --> 00:59:20.500
We learned that the relationship between
assets then, whether measured in terms of
covariance or correlation,

00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:24.500
is the most important factor in portfolio
diversification.

00:59:24.500 --> 00:59:29.500
With that in mind then, it set us up with
the key concept running throughout this
video,

00:59:29.500 --> 00:59:36.500
which has been that correlation assessment
is a hugely important factor to consider
in the context of portfolio construction.

00:59:36.500 --> 00:59:40.500
And I've talked through and demonstrated
that in a number of ways.

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:46.500
We've discussed how efficient
diversification leads to higher risk
adjusted returns.

00:59:46.500 --> 01:00:01.500
What I mean by efficient diversification
is incorporating positions in the
portfolio with low correlations to reduce
portfolio risk at a faster rate and a
higher magnitude than if we included
medium or high correlation positions.

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:11.500
That allows us to sufficiently diversify
without including so many positions that
we then sacrifice the quality and
conviction of our ideas,

01:00:11.500 --> 01:00:15.500
which is obviously detrimental to our
expected returns.

01:00:15.500 --> 01:00:22.500
Remember also that the more positions you
add, the lower the marginal benefit of
risk reduction becomes anyway.

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:32.500
So building a long short portfolio that
predominantly incorporates mid cap stocks
is a great vehicle to actually achieve
this.

01:00:32.500 --> 01:00:38.500
A mix of long and short trades is a
massive aid to lower correlation positions
and hence reduce risk.

01:00:38.500 --> 01:00:44.500
And mid caps not only offer up the
opportunity for higher profits than larger
cap stocks,

01:00:44.500 --> 01:00:48.500
but also give us a wider range of
correlations to work with.

01:00:48.500 --> 01:00:55.500
And often they'll represent a more
efficient diversifier than their larger
cap counterparts as well.

01:00:55.500 --> 01:01:00.500
So with that being said, there's a few
things to consider at this point from a
practical perspective.

01:01:00.500 --> 01:01:06.500
There are many considerations in using
correlation values as I covered throughout
the first section of this video.

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:12.500
And it would be naive to assume that
historical correlation always implies
future correlation,

01:01:12.500 --> 01:01:17.500
since there may be events in the future
that cause correlations to change, even if
only temporarily.

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:20.500
So we've seen that in a number of examples
throughout the video.

01:01:20.500 --> 01:01:29.500
One of the most important ones being when
we looked at what happens when liquidity
crises occur and correlations tend to one.

01:01:29.500 --> 01:01:35.500
There's a sort of dash for cash and all
assets tend to move in the same direction.

01:01:35.500 --> 01:01:37.500
So correlations tend to one.

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:41.500
I've already touched on the idea of over
diversification as well,

01:01:41.500 --> 01:01:44.500
but that is another important practical
consideration.

01:01:44.500 --> 01:01:53.500
Because even in the unrealistic scenario
where we assume we can find an endless
number of low correlation ideas to add to
the portfolio,

01:01:53.500 --> 01:01:58.500
we still need to be cognizant of the
opportunity or profit side of the
equation.

01:01:58.500 --> 01:02:05.500
And what I mean by that is that over
diversification tends to lead to executing
lower quality and lower conviction ideas,

01:02:05.500 --> 01:02:08.500
and that can seriously damage future
returns.

01:02:08.500 --> 01:02:17.500
So there still needs to be a certain
amount of concentration of positions to
maximize returns whilst taking advantage
of low correlations where possible.

01:02:17.500 --> 01:02:26.500
And for the umpteenth time, correlation
considerations always come after solid
fundamental analysis.

01:02:26.500 --> 01:02:32.500
Another thing I want to reiterate from the
previous video is that by constructing a
long short portfolio,

01:02:32.500 --> 01:02:37.500
we've seen that estimated portfolio
volatility decreases significantly.

01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:39.500
Despite the reduction in volatility,

01:02:39.500 --> 01:02:46.500
it's important to remember that that
doesn't necessarily mean less opportunity
for profit.

01:02:46.500 --> 01:02:51.500
Long short portfolio strategies can work
in all markets,

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:54.500
which actually is much better for wealth
